    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 1 (Questions 1&ndash;6)</div>\n<p>In his landmark 1964 essay &ldquo;Rhetoric of the Image,&rdquo; French semiotician Roland Barthes mounted a decisive assault on the widespread cultural assumption that visual images, particularly photographs, serve as neutral, unmediated windows onto objective reality. Confronting the mid-twentieth-century proliferation of mass commercial advertising, Barthes recognized that visual representations do not simply reproduce raw empirical facts; rather, they obscure their own ideological construction behind an illusion of effortless naturalness. To systematically expose how visual communication manufactures meaning, Barthes decomposed the visual artifact into three distinct communicative layers: the linguistic message, the coded iconic (or connotative) message, and the non-coded iconic (or denotative) message.</p>\n<p>To demonstrate this taxonomy, Barthes conducted his classic semiotic reading of a print advertisement for Panzani pasta. At the denotative level, the viewer observes an ostensibly literal scene: a half-open mesh string shopping bag spilling dry pasta packets, tins of sauce, fresh ripe tomatoes, and yellow onions onto a neutral surface. Unlike manual illustration or painting, which visibly requires stylistic rules of transposition and expressive intervention, mechanical photography operates through an analogical capture of physical light. Barthes famously characterized this as a &ldquo;message without a code,&rdquo; because the photographic signifier appears completely identical to its signified referent. Consequently, this denotative plane presents itself as innocent, objective, and self-evident. However, Barthes argued that this apparent innocence functions as a sophisticated ideological alibi. Grafted directly onto the literal denotation is a constellation of cultural connotations: the green, white, and red color scheme subtly recalls the Italian national tricolor; the loose, unbagged produce signifies the pastoral freshness of agrarian open-air markets; and the phonetic euphony of the brand name itself crystallizes what Barthes termed &ldquo;Italianicity,&rdquo; an idealized bourgeois stereotype of Mediterranean culinary authenticity.</p>\n<p>In deciphering the relationship between words and pictures, Barthes identified two primary functions of the linguistic text: <i>anchorage</i> and <i>relay</i>. Because visual signs are inherently polysemic&mdash;pregnant with an unstable, floating multiplicity of potential meanings&mdash;the spectator faces an interpretive ambiguity that threatens commercial clarity. Anchorage occurs when a caption, label, or headline fixes the polysemic ambiguity of the visual signs, effectively selecting and privileging a single predetermined reading while repressing subversive alternatives. This operation is equally paramount in photojournalism, where a caption instantly classifies a photograph of a gathering crowd as either a celebratory civic assembly or an unruly riot. Relay, by contrast, appears predominantly in cinema and comic strips, where verbal dialogue and visual action operate in reciprocal complementarity, advancing the narrative by alternating narrative duties. Under modern advertising, anchorage reigns supreme, functioning as a disciplinary mechanism that guides the consumer toward the desired commercial mythology.</p>\n<p>The ultimate political objective of commercial semiotics, Barthes contended, is the naturalization of history. By anchoring bourgeois commercial values in the literal, mechanical reality of the photograph&rsquo;s denotation, the image makes contingent cultural fictions appear as universal, immutable facts of nature. Yet subsequent cultural theorists have contested Barthes&rsquo;s formulation. Postmodern critics such as Jean Baudrillard argued that Barthes remained trapped in an outdated semiotic nostalgia for an authentic referent, asserting that contemporary media environments generate free-floating simulacra where the distinction between denotation and connotation completely collapses. Furthermore, reception theorists contend that Barthes ascribed excessive determinism to the visual text, underestimating the capacity of oppositional audiences to subvert commercial codes through resistant decodings. Nevertheless, Barthes&rsquo;s foundational framework retains profound relevance, illuminating how modern culture continually masks its economic imperatives behind a facade of visual innocence.</p>",
