    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>In classical rhetoric, <i>ekphrasis</i> originally denoted the vivid verbal representation of a visual object, compelling the mind&rsquo;s eye of the listener to behold what language could only describe. By the European Renaissance and the Romantic era, however, ekphrasis had evolved from an illustrative rhetorical exercise into an intense aesthetic <i>paragone</i>&mdash;a competitive rivalry between the temporal medium of poetry and the spatial medium of plastic art. Gotthold Ephraim Lessing, in his landmark 1766 aesthetic treatise <i>Laoco&ouml;n</i>, famously sought to police the rigid ontological boundaries between these sister arts, asserting that painting and sculpture operate in space through coexistent bodies, whereas poetry operates across time through successive actions. Yet it was the English Romantic poet John Keats who, in his 1819 masterpiece &ldquo;Ode on a Grecian Urn,&rdquo; staged the most profound meditation on this ontological duel, testing whether poetic language could celebrate, emulate, and ultimately surpass the silent, immutable permanence of sculpted marble.</p>\n<p>Throughout the ode, Keats addresses the ancient artifact with an intoxicating mixture of sensual reverie and probing philosophical inquiry, hailing it as an &ldquo;unravish&rsquo;d bride of quietness&rdquo; and a &ldquo;foster-child of silence and slow time.&rdquo; The urn presents an idyllic realm suspended in perpetual perfection: marble lovers poised on the verge of an eternal kiss, arboreal boughs that will never shed their leaves, and youthful musicians playing pipes whose silent melodies surpass the sensory limitations of mortal ears (&ldquo;Heard melodies are sweet, but those unheard / Are sweeter&rdquo;). For a mortal poet intensely conscious of physical fragility and civilizational transience, the urn&rsquo;s plastic immutability appears initially as a glorious triumph over biological decay. Its sculpted inhabitants reside outside the agonizing flux of human passion, which leaves behind a &ldquo;heart high-sorrowful and cloy&rsquo;d, / A burning forehead, and a parching tongue.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Yet Keats&rsquo;s ekphrastic tribute is fraught with profound philosophical ambivalence, pivoting from ecstatic celebration to an unsettling realization of plastic art&rsquo;s existential sterility. The very stasis that confers immortality upon the marble figures simultaneously deprives them of genuine vitality, growth, and ethical warmth. The lover can never consummate his desire; the pipers can never alter their melody; and the mysterious emptied town from which the sacrificial procession originated remains forever desolate, devoid of any soul to explain its abandonment. By famously apostrophizing the vessel as a &ldquo;Cold Pastoral,&rdquo; Keats shatters the idyllic pastoral illusion. The marble&rsquo;s permanence is revealed as stone coldness, indifferent to the mortal suffering that surrounds it. The ekphrastic rivalry demonstrates that while visual plastic art possesses enduring spatial presence, it remains fundamentally mute, requiring the temporal, breath-borne medium of poetic language to endow its silent marble with narrative meaning and emotional resonance.</p>\n<p>This hermeneutic tension reaches its enigmatic climax in the poem&rsquo;s concluding lines, where the urn delivers its famous oracle: &ldquo;&lsquo;Beauty is truth, truth beauty,&rsquo;&mdash;that is all / Ye know on earth, and all ye need to know.&rdquo; For two centuries, literary critics have engaged in impassioned disputes regarding this couplet, with T.S. Eliot notoriously dismissing it as an unearned, grammatically meaningless blemish that mars an otherwise great poem. However, when understood through the lens of ekphrasis, the aphorism represents neither shallow sentimentality nor philosophical carelessness. Rather, it exposes the unbridgeable chasm separating aesthetic self-containment from mortal human experience. The urn offers a teasing, enigmatic closure that consoles while withholding historical truth, proving that the rivalry between word and stone cannot end in neat reconciliation, but in an enduring dialectic between the immortal stillness of plastic art and the ephemeral, suffering consciousness of the human voice.</p>",
