    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 9 (Questions 49&ndash;53)</div>\n<p>In February 1897, British military forces launched a devastating punitive expedition against the Kingdom of Benin in modern-day southern Nigeria. Organized under the official diplomatic pretext of avenging a slain British delegation, the campaign served the broader imperial objective of shattering the kingdom&rsquo;s independent commercial monopoly over palm oil and rubber trade. The British soldiers captured and burned the historic royal palace, deposed and exiled the Oba (king) Ovonramwen, and systematically looted over three thousand intricate cast brass and bronze relief plaques, carved ivory tusks, and ancestral ceremonial sculptures dating from the thirteenth through sixteenth centuries. Auctioned by the British Admiralty to offset the operational costs of the military campaign, these masterpieces&mdash;collectively known as the Benin Bronzes&mdash;were rapidly dispersed among Western ethnological and national museums, with the largest single collection anchoring the British Museum in London. Today, the Benin Bronzes stand as the primary global flashpoint in the contentious debate over the colonial provenance of museum antiquities and the ethical imperative of cultural repatriation.</p>\n<p>Defending their permanent retention of looted treasures, major Western institutions have historically rallied around the doctrine of the &ldquo;encyclopedic museum.&rdquo; Formalized in the 2002 &ldquo;Declaration on the Importance and Value of Universal Museums&rdquo; signed by eighteen major international institutions, this ideology posits that metropolitan museums serve as cosmopolitan repositories for the collective heritage of all humanity. Proponents argue that by displaying artifacts from diverse global civilizations alongside one another, universal museums cultivate cross-cultural dialogue and historical appreciation that transcend narrow nationalist boundaries. Furthermore, defenders deploy paternalistic custodial arguments, contending that Western institutions possess superior conservation technologies, security infrastructure, and scholarly resources to safeguard delicate artifacts against political instability, armed conflict, or environmental decay in originating countries.</p>\n<p>Decolonial theorists, international legal scholars, and originating communities reject the universal museum defense as an intellectual rebranding of imperial plunder. Critics emphasize that the &ldquo;universalism&rdquo; championed by institutions like the British Museum is inherently asymmetric and hypocritical: it represents an imperial cosmopolitanism constructed through violent conquest, wherein Western colonial powers unilaterally designated themselves as the self-appointed stewards of cultures they subjugated. To celebrate cross-cultural dialogue while refusing to return stolen patrimony constitutes an ongoing act of epistemic and political violence. For the Edo people of Benin, the Bronzes were never conceived as static aesthetic commodities for detached optical spectatorship. Rather, they functioned as living spiritual chronologies, dynastic ancestral altars, and sacred historical archives; their violent extraction decapitated the kingdom&rsquo;s ceremonial life and communal memory.</p>\n<p>In recent years, sustained diplomatic pressure, decolonial activism, and the groundbreaking 2018 Sarr-Savoy report have begun to erode institutional resistance across Europe. Commissioned by the French presidency, the Sarr-Savoy report highlighted that over ninety percent of sub-Saharan Africa&rsquo;s tangible cultural heritage resides in European depositories, recommending the permanent restitution of all objects acquired through non-consensual colonial expropriation. While nations such as Germany have initiated the unconditional legal transfer of hundreds of Benin Bronzes to Nigerian sovereignty, institutions like the British Museum continue to resist full repatriation, often hiding behind domestic statutory prohibitions like the British Museum Act of 1963 and proposing temporary rotating loans instead. Originating communities rightly reject loans of their own looted heritage as a patronizing colonial insult. True museum decolonization requires moving beyond paternalistic stewardship toward an ethics of restorative justice: recognizing that cultural sovereignty is indivisible, and that a modern institution cannot profess universal humanism while holding captive the violently stolen sacred history of another nation.</p>",
